    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "This is a compelling defining statement for Kirk: a cohesive, blues-rooted studio album in which his multi-instrumental reed vocabulary serves emotionally substantial compositions. The dossier supplies meaningful cross-tradition support: AllMusic 5-star, Penguin 3.5-star, and Pitchfork #170 on 200 Best Albums of the 1960s. It documents the distinctive quartet with Rahn Burton, Steve Novosel, and Jimmy Hopps, plus Dick Griffin on trombone. The Ellington connection, accessible songfulness, and vivid instrumental personality give it strong discovery value and preserve Kirk's singular place in post-bop history.",
    "case_against": "The case is stronger as a canonical Kirk album than as an indispensable jazz-album landmark: the dossier lacks NPR, DownBeat, or JazzTimes canon evidence, and Penguin's 3.5-star rating falls short of its highest tier. Kirk's competing landmark Rip, Rig and Panic has greater session gravity through Jaki Byard, Richard Davis, and Elvin Jones. The sourcing is adequate but shallow for some claims, with track-to-session mappings incomplete.",
    "disagreement": "The references split on tier: Reference 1 argues contested/strong while Reference 2 treats it as consensus_core/must_have. The boundary is whether Kirk's definitive personal statement alone makes the album core to the broader jazz canon.",
